        "id": "spring-cycle",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Spring: renewal and the new year",
        "content": "Nowruz, meaning “new day,” marks the spring new year. Its geographical reach extends beyond Iran, connecting communities across Central Asia, the Caucasus and other regions. The United Nations recognizes 21 March as International Nowruz Day; the festival itself is associated with the astronomical spring equinox. Those are related reference points, rather than a reason to assign every local observance an identical timetable. [s1]\n\nChaharshanbe Suri belongs to the approach to Nowruz. Its principal celebration takes place on the evening before the final Wednesday of the Iranian solar year. Fire is its best-known element, but the scholarly record also documents other customs and local names. It deserves attention as a distinct occasion within the spring festive cycle. [s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "autumn-winter",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Harvest, darkness and the return of warmth",
        "content": "Mehregan connects celebration with the autumn harvest. UNESCO’s account of practices in Iran and Tajikistan describes tables of produce, prepared dishes, music and performances, alongside religious observances that differ between practitioners. It should therefore not be presented simply as a uniform secular harvest party. The products displayed and the rituals performed depend on the community concerned. [s5]\n\nYalda, also called Chella, takes place on the last night of autumn. UNESCO describes families gathering in elders’ homes, with poetry, storytelling, games and food. Red fruits, nuts and sweets contribute to the gathering, while the occasion provides a setting for passing on family knowledge. Its importance lies as much in spending time together as in the objects arranged on a table. [s3]\n\nSadeh has a different winter setting. UNESCO describes a celebration involving fire and activities associated with preparation for the coming agricultural season. Its account includes communal maintenance of waterways and bridges after the celebration. This connection between festivity and practical cooperation broadens the picture beyond the spectacle of a bonfire. [s4]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s3",
          "s4",
          "s5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "transmission-and-variation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How celebrations continue",
        "content": "Traditions continue through participation: helping prepare food, listening to stories and learning how a gathering is organized. UNESCO’s Yalda, Sadeh and Mehregan descriptions also identify contemporary channels of transmission, including educational institutions, publications or media. Family practice and organized cultural activity can therefore work alongside one another. Continuity does not require every detail to remain unchanged. [s3][s4][s5]\n\nRegional comparison should ask specific questions: which foods are prepared, who hosts, which stories are told, and how agricultural or household work enters the occasion? These questions are more revealing than treating a national festival name as a complete description. The pages in this section introduce the major occasions while leaving room for local accounts and differences within communities. No participation percentages or rankings are supplied: the inspected sources describe practices, not a comparable national survey of their prevalence. [s2][s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": [
