    "content": [
      {
        "id": "new-day",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the new day marks",
        "content": "Nowruz means “new day.” It links the beginning of the year with spring’s return and is celebrated across a region extending well beyond Iran. The United Nations presents it as a shared tradition associated with renewal and relationships between generations. Gathering for a festive meal and exchanging gifts, especially with children, are among the practices described in its account. These provide an introduction to the occasion without reducing its many regional forms to a single national template. [s1]\n\nThe spring equinox is the astronomical reference point. International Nowruz Day, recognized by the United Nations in 2010, is fixed on 21 March. That international observance should be distinguished from the precise astronomical timing of the seasonal event. [s1]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "household-display",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading a haft-sin display",
        "content": "A familiar Iranian expression of Nowruz is haft sin: an arrangement named for seven items beginning with the Persian letter sin, pronounced like the English “s.” Encyclopaedia Iranica describes sprouted grain, apples, garlic, vinegar and samanu, a sweet preparation made with sprouted wheat, among its components. The display may stand on a cloth spread on the floor or on a table. Mirrors, flowers and other objects can accompany the named items. [s2]\n\nThe arrangement is better understood as a family of practices than as an immutable checklist. Iranica records additional items and differences in usage, as well as the inclusion of literary works such as the Shahnameh, the Persian epic, or the poetry of Hafez. Its account also cautions that the display’s precise origins are obscure: ancient uses of grain or symbolic groupings do not by themselves prove that the modern haft-sin arrangement existed unchanged in antiquity. [s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-layers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An old celebration with a changing history",
        "content": "Nowruz has a central place in Zoroastrian tradition, the religious tradition of ancient Iran that remains a living faith. Historian Mary Boyce distinguishes religious and secular strands, both of which developed over many centuries. She also notes a significant evidential limit: the name Nowruz does not occur in the surviving Old or Young Avestan texts, but appears in Middle Persian texts. Historical reconstruction therefore requires more care than assigning the entire modern celebration a single ancient starting date. [s4]\n\nThe festival continued after the Islamic conquest. Alireza Shapur Shahbazi’s historical survey describes its adaptation under successive courts and its role in Iranian cultural memory. Royal ceremonies, including gift exchange and formal audiences, could display a ruler’s prestige; they were not simply enlarged versions of a private family gathering. The same survey warns that the evidence is uneven, assembled from chronicles, poetry, travellers’ accounts and later studies. [s3]\n\nFor a reader encountering Nowruz today, that layered history is essential. The celebration can carry religious significance, cultural belonging and the pleasures of a shared new year. Its longevity is compatible with adaptation, and the history of one custom should not be treated as the history of every other custom associated with it. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s3",
          "s4"
        ]
      }
    ],
