        "id": "timing-and-name",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why a Wednesday celebration begins on Tuesday",
        "content": "Chaharshanbe means Wednesday, but the principal celebration begins on Tuesday evening: the eve of the final Wednesday of the Iranian solar year. It belongs to the approach to Nowruz rather than the new year’s opening day. The meaning of suri is debated, with explanations connecting it to redness or festivity. Both associations fit the occasion, but the name alone cannot establish its historical origin. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "fire-and-interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Fire, colour and wishes for renewal",
        "content": "Encyclopaedia Iranica describes people jumping over burning brushwood while addressing the fire in words that exchange their paleness for its redness. In the recorded symbolism, paleness evokes illness and weakness, while redness represents vigour. These are descriptions of ritual belief, rather than claims that fire prevents disease. [s1]\n\nThe history requires a distinction between older Iranian fire celebrations and the particular customs associated with this Wednesday evening. Iranica discusses possible antecedents but cautions that an early account of a year-end fire does not describe all the later rituals. It is therefore misleading to present every modern feature as an unchanged ancient practice. Older elements, later interpretations and local customs can coexist within the same celebration. [s1]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "beyond-bonfires",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Food and neighbourhood customs",
        "content": "The evening encompasses more than fire. Persian Language Online describes qashoq-zani, or spoon-banging, in which children make noise with kitchen utensils. It also records kuze-shekani, the ritual breaking of a pot, as a symbolic departure from misfortune. Such descriptions explain recorded customs; they should not be read as instructions to reproduce potentially hazardous actions. [s2]\n\nFood provides another way into the occasion. The same account describes regional preparations of ash, a substantial soup, and a mixture of dried fruit and nuts called ajil-e moshkel-gosha. Its name associates it with overcoming difficulties. Sharing food places the celebration within household and neighbourhood relationships, alongside its more conspicuous outdoor activities. [s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-reading",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A celebration with local forms",
        "content": "Regional descriptions resist a single national script. Persian Language Online records gift trays sent between families in Iranian Azerbaijan and water-related customs in Bushehr on the Persian Gulf coast. These are examples from particular settings, not evidence that everyone in either region performs them today. [s2]\n\nThe most useful way to understand Chaharshanbe Suri is to hold its shared seasonal setting and its local differences together. Its recurring themes include leaving misfortune behind and approaching the new year with renewed vitality. The available descriptions explain those meanings more securely than they establish how common each custom is across contemporary Iran. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      }
    ],
